## EXPORTING WEBGL BUILD

1. **Open File > Build Settings…** in Unity
   - Ensure that the `Start_SplashScreen` scene is in the first (0) position in the build list. 
   - Choose WebGL as the platform and click `Switch Platform` to load the WebGL settings
   - Open the Build Settings and set the **resolution** to
     - Minimum of **640x385** or a Maximum of **1280x720** to ensure a 16:9 format that fits the webpage. 
   - Choose `Build and Run` to build and preview the game
     - Create a `WebGLBuild` folder to save the build files, **this should be outside the Unity project folder**
   - Test the game in the run preview, **verifying that all features are working correctly**.

> [!NOTE]
The first scene in the build list must be the game's starting scene, which in our case is `Start_SplashScreen`. This will load by default. Since scene navigation is handled dynamically by referencing scene names, the order of the remaining scenes in the build list is irrelevant. You can reorder scenes by simply dragging and dropping them. Additionally, remove any unneeded scenes from the build list to optimize performance and reduce file size.

2. Zip the contents of the `WebGLBuild` folder
   - Open `WebGLBuild` folder
   - Select all the contents of the folder, including the internal `Build` folder and other files. 
   - Zip the selected content, and name the zip using the assignment naming standards. 
     - For example, if you completed the project on Oct 15th and your last name is Dent, then the zip file should be named `2024-10-15_Dent_PuzzleGame.zip`

> [!Warning]
> Build files are typically large and not suited for GitHub. Unity’s `.gitignore` also excludes build files by default. Usually, these builds are saved elsewhere, but for this small project, we're placing the `WebGLBuild` folder in the root of the repository to keep related files together. This bypasses the ignore rule, though it’s not recommended. Additionally, when compressing for itch.io, avoid zipping the entire folder, as Windows creates an extra subfolder that itch.io won’t recognize. Instead, select the folder’s contents before zipping.

